            <ol><li><strong>Collect the numbers.</strong> You need date of birth and date of assessment. Write each one with its unit before you type it.</li><li><strong>Put them in.</strong> Press Calculate when the boxes are full; the panel updates again if you change a value.</li><li><strong>Sanity-check the size.</strong> Years and months and days and chronological age in days should be in the range you expected before you pressed anything. Ten times out is a unit slip; twice out is usually a wrong assumption.</li><li><strong>Change one thing.</strong> Move a single input and confirm the answer moves the way the formula says it should. Then the number is yours to use.</li></ol><ul><li><li>Filling in a developmental assessment form.</li></li><li><li>Recording an age at an examination date.</li></li></ul><p>Close neighbours on this site: <a href="/everyday-life/age-in-years-months-and-days">Years, months and days</a> <a href="/everyday-life/age">Age calculator</a> <a href="/everyday-life/baby-age">Baby age</a>.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'If the answer looks wrong', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Check the size first, then the units, then the inputs. A number that is out by exactly ten or a hundred is a unit slip; a number that is out by two is usually an assumption, such as counting both ends of a date range or treating an average as a constant. Only after those two checks is it worth suspecting the arithmetic, which is one line long and has been checked against the worked example above.</p><p>If you are comparing this page with another tool and the figures differ slightly, the difference is usually rounding: this page computes at full precision and shortens only for display, while a hand calculation often rounds at every step and drifts.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'When one number is out by a factor of ten', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>A decimal slip leaves an answer that still looks like an answer. Here is what the first result does when each box is exaggerated:</p><table class="calc-table"><thead><tr><th>Change</th><th>Result</th><th>Becomes</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Date of birth</strong> +10 days</td><td>36</td><td>36</td><td>unchanged</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Date of assessment</strong> +10 days</td><td>36</td><td>36</td><td>unchanged</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Where the effect column says ten times larger, that box feeds the answer directly, so a slip passes straight through. Where the effect is smaller, the box sits inside a sum or a ratio and the mistake is partly absorbed. That is worse, not better: the answer then looks believable.</p>
            TXT],
